## Question 2: Circular Queue Using an Array

A **queue** follows the **FIFO** (First In, First Out) rule: the first item enqueued is the first item dequeued. A circular queue wraps its rear index to the start of the array when it reaches the last position. This implementation tracks the number of items, so it can distinguish a full queue from an empty one.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class CircularQueue {
private:
    static const int CAPACITY = 5;
    int items[CAPACITY];
    int frontIndex;
    int rearIndex;
    int count;

public:
    CircularQueue() : frontIndex(0), rearIndex(-1), count(0) {}

    bool isEmpty() const {
        return count == 0;
    }

    bool isFull() const {
        return count == CAPACITY;
    }

    void enqueue(int value) {
        if (isFull()) {
            cout << "Queue is full; cannot enqueue " << value << ".\n";
            return;
        }

        rearIndex = (rearIndex + 1) % CAPACITY;
        items[rearIndex] = value;
        ++count;
        cout << value << " enqueued.\n";
    }

    void dequeue() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            cout << "Queue is empty; cannot dequeue.\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << items[frontIndex] << " dequeued.\n";
        frontIndex = (frontIndex + 1) % CAPACITY;
        --count;

        if (count == 0) {
            frontIndex = 0;
            rearIndex = -1;
        }
    }

    void front() const {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            cout << "Queue is empty; there is no front element.\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << "Front element: " << items[frontIndex] << '\n';
    }

    void display() const {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            cout << "Queue is empty.\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << "Queue (front to rear): ";
        for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
            int index = (frontIndex + i) % CAPACITY;
            cout << items[index] << ' ';
        }
        cout << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    CircularQueue queue;

    queue.enqueue(10);
    queue.enqueue(20);
    queue.enqueue(30);
    queue.display();
    queue.front();

    queue.dequeue();
    queue.enqueue(40);
    queue.enqueue(50);
    queue.display();

    return 0;
}
```

### Circular queue comparison and complexity

1. **Memory utilization:** A circular queue reuses positions freed by dequeues. When the rear reaches the last array index, it wraps to the beginning if space is available.
2. **Time complexity:** `ENQUEUE` and `DEQUEUE` each take **O(1)** time because they update an index and the item count without shifting other elements.
3. **Space complexity:** The array uses **O(CAPACITY)** space. The queue uses **O(1)** extra space for its indices and count.
4. **Linear queue limitation:** In a simple array-based linear queue, the rear can reach the last index even after dequeues have freed positions at the beginning. The queue may then appear full despite unused array positions. This is called **false overflow** or wasted space. A circular queue reuses those positions by wrapping around.

